From 53c13a8bef39b480fb887be77cbed4602cadb63f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 20/32] skippy: sample a batch's requests concurrently

An iteration's sampled requests waited for one another: after the batch's
outputs were recorded, each request ran its sampler chain in turn on the
scheduler thread while the GPU sat idle. With four requests decoding
together on Qwen3.5-4B, sampling took about 0.5 ms of every ~21 ms step,
most of it the top-k pass over the full 248,320-token vocabulary.

skippy_iteration_batch_sampled now records every request's outputs first and
then samples the requests together. Its sessions are already distinct and
share one context. A sampler that would attach or retire a backend sampler
on that context, or that samples through one, is readied and sampled first
on the calling thread. The rest sample at the same time, one thread each,
under a skippy.sample_concurrent span that their skippy.sample spans join.

Sampling reads the context's outputs, and every read synchronizes the
context and applies pending output reorders, which write to it. A
llama_synchronized_outputs, made only by llama_context::synchronize_outputs
after doing both, lets a thread hold a llama_output_reader; while it does,
that thread's reads of the context skip both steps, so concurrent readers
write nothing shared.

skippy-concurrent-sampling decodes four lanes together for twelve steps on
dense and hybrid recurrent fixtures, and checks every sampled token against
four more sessions with the same seeded samplers sampling the same rows one
after another, and that all four sampled under the concurrent span.

